WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.160
A thirsty mouse starts to drink.

2
00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.340
Within a minute, its thirst neurons go quiet.

3
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.260
Yet its blood osmolality is still raised.

4
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.180
What are they reading?

5
00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:14.893
Start with a loop.

6
00:00:15.793 --> 00:00:20.673
For water, the thing held steady is plasma osmolality, the concentration of

7
00:00:20.673 --> 00:00:22.253
dissolved particles in plasma.

8
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:28.043
The SFO and OVLT sit in the front wall of the third ventricle.

9
00:00:28.723 --> 00:00:32.503
They sample the blood directly, where the blood-brain barrier is incomplete.

10
00:00:33.423 --> 00:00:37.543
A third site behind the barrier, the MnPO, integrates their signals.

11
00:00:38.617 --> 00:00:40.197
Two effectors push back.

12
00:00:41.077 --> 00:00:43.057
Vasopressin saves water at the kidney.

13
00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:45.137
Thirst takes water in.

14
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:47.587
Both lower osmolality.

15
00:00:48.447 --> 00:00:51.607
The response opposes the error, so this is negative feedback.

16
00:00:52.667 --> 00:00:55.467
Is there a separate comparator that judges the error?

17
00:00:56.287 --> 00:00:59.927
The comparison is spread across the sensing and integrating neurons.

18
00:01:00.847 --> 00:01:03.567
We summarize the result with a threshold and a gain.

19
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.720
Raise osmolality in a controlled way and record vasopressin.

20
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.880
The plot is flat, then rises in a straight line.

21
00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:14.370
The knee is the threshold.

22
00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:16.490
It says when release starts.

23
00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:18.410
The slope is the gain.

24
00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:20.730
It says how much release grows.

25
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.240
Suppose osmolality rises, but stays below threshold.

26
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.380
Does vasopressin change?

27
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.520
No. Below threshold there is no osmotic release.

28
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:39.860
The line does not go negative.

29
00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:42.433
Now two made-up people.

30
00:01:43.513 --> 00:01:45.153
Person P has the lower threshold.

31
00:01:46.173 --> 00:01:47.853
Person Q has the steeper gain.

32
00:01:48.867 --> 00:01:51.667
Just above P's threshold, who releases more?

33
00:01:52.187 --> 00:01:54.007
And far above both thresholds?

34
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.260
Just above P's threshold, P does.

35
00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:04.540
Q has not even started.

36
00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.780
Far above both, Q does.

37
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:08.680
The steeper line wins.

38
00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.730
So one number, a set point, cannot describe the loop.

39
00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:14.570
It takes at least two.

40
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.370
For osmotic vasopressin, the line is measured.

41
00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:22.890
Output is the gain, times how far osmolality is past threshold.

42
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.840
But water-balance circuits do not read the blood alone.

43
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.660
Think of them as acting on an estimate.

44
00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:31.720
Feedback reads the blood.

45
00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:35.420
Feedforward adds what was just swallowed, or a learned cue.

46
00:02:36.433 --> 00:02:39.693
The hypothalamus acts through nerves, often in seconds.

47
00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:44.733
It also acts through hormones, whose effects usually build over minutes to hours.

48
00:02:45.817 --> 00:02:47.217
Threshold says when.

49
00:02:48.217 --> 00:02:49.217
Gain says how much.

50
00:02:49.877 --> 00:02:50.877
Keep the word estimate.

51
00:02:51.777 --> 00:02:53.297
The thirst neurons will show why.
